Here’s some areas we should tackle to include on the company website:

About Us 

Clients want to know who they’re hiring.

Include:
    •    Company story (why you exist)
    •    Years of experience
    •    Core values (quality, safety, integrity, timelines)
    •    Leadership / owner bio
    •    Certifications or training
    •    What makes you different

Portfolio / Projects

This is one of the most important pages.

Include:
    •    High-quality photos (before & after if possible)
    •    Project description
    •    Location (city only, not exact address)
    •    Type of project
    •    Timeline & scope
    •    Challenges solved

Services Page 

Break services into clear sections.

Examples:
    •    General contracting
    •    Residential construction
    •    Commercial construction
    •    Renovations / remodels
    •    Design-build
    •    Project management
    •    Demolition / site prep
    •    Custom homes
    •    Tenant improvements

For each service, include:
    •    Short description
    •    Type of clients served
    •    Typical project size or scope

Licensing, Insurance & Credentials (Critical)

This instantly builds credibility.
    •    Business license numbers
    •    Contractor license
    •    Insurance coverage (general liability, WSIB, etc.)
    •    Bonded (if applicable)
    •    Certifications (LEED, Red Seal, PMP, etc.)
    •    Association memberships

Process (How You Work)

This sets expectations and shows professionalism.

Example:
    1.    Consultation
    2.    Site visit & assessment
    3.    Estimate & timeline
    4.    Permits & planning
    5.    Construction
    6.    Final inspection & handover

Contact Page (Simple & Strong)

Make it easy.

Include:
    •    Contact form
    •    Phone number
    •    Email
    •    Office address (if applicable)
    •    Business hours
    •    Service areas map (optional)